+- [#68](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/68) [`c164fee`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/c164fee480a36fa5f70885d80df1a404ce3b5764) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - Studio: a picture's `url()` left with an open quote in a page's changes no longer stalls the editor.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - Footage is decoded on the graphics chip on every system: VideoToolbox on a Mac, Direct3D 11 (then NVIDIA's decoder) on Windows, and VA-API (then NVIDIA's) on Linux. This covers the fast-seeking copies, the preview's proxies and the files an editing project converts. Each decoder is tried once on a moment of H.264 and used only when its frames match the processor's pixel for pixel. If it fails partway, the work is done again without it.
+
+  Footage the render's browser can't decode no longer stalls an export: ProRes, which no browser plays, and HEVC on a computer without a chip for it. Before rendering, such footage is converted to a copy the browser plays, keeping its transparency when it has one. A film with ProRes footage used to take about thirty seconds a frame and showed none of it.
+
+  `openfilm doctor` says which encoders and decoders ffmpeg uses on the chip, and which video codecs the browser decodes there.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - A diagnostic report to paste into an issue. `openfilm doctor` checks what OpenFilm needs on this computer: Node, ffmpeg, the browser films are drawn in and whether it uses the graphics chip, Studio, the data and projects folders, and npm. It also shows which agent ran the command and the last errors in Studio's log. Settings → General → Copy report copies the same report, plus what the browser Studio is open in can do. In the desktop app, it adds each agent's program, version and state. Paths in your home folder show as ~; the report has no keys, accounts or projects.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - Diagnostics move to Settings → Developer, which Studio now shows outside the desktop app too. Studio and the desktop app keep what went wrong (an agent that did not start or answer, or whose process ended by itself; an export that failed; an error Studio did not expect) in `logs/problems.jsonl` in Studio's home: the last two weeks, at most 500, with home paths, keys, tokens and email addresses taken out. The copied report (and `openfilm doctor`) ends with the last week's problems. Export writes a diagnostic file to the exports folder: the report, the problems, the end of each log, how the failed turns went (what was said only if you ask for it), and Codex's own warnings and errors around its problems. Settings → Developer lists the recent problems. In the desktop app, a message that failed has "Copy diagnostic report" beside its error, and so does an agent with a problem in Settings → Agents.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - Studio: a file dragged from the media pane onto the timeline is put down where you let go, or on a new track when that spot is taken. It never cuts, pushes or covers a clip there; hold ⌥ to overwrite.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - Exports leave the computer usable, and do less work twice:
+
+  - **Fewer browsers.** A render opens one browser per performance core minus one, as many as the memory free right now can hold with 2 GB to spare, and at most four. Before, a Mac with 16 GB opened five and could run out of memory. A short render opens one.
+  - **Lower priority.** Exports and `openfilm render` run below the apps you're using, along with every browser and encoder they start. This now holds in the desktop app too.
+  - **Your film's frame rate.** A platform preset exports at the film's own rate, up to the most the platform shows. A 30 fps film is no longer drawn at 60 for 哔哩哔哩 or YouTube.
+  - **One render for same-size presets.** Platforms that take the same picture, such as 抖音, 快手 and 视频号, are drawn once and their files cloned.
+  - **Faster PNG frames.** Alpha videos and editing-project exports take their PNG frames with fast compression. The pixels are the same, and the frames come two to four times sooner.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - Exports leave more of the work to the Mac's media engines, so the computer stays usable while they run. ProRes exports, and the pages an editing project carries, go through the ProRes engine, about three times as fast with a quarter of the processor. `openfilm render` writes H.264 on the graphics chip, as Studio's exports already do. The copies a render makes of slow-seeking footage, and the preview's proxies of heavy footage, are decoded and encoded on the chip too. H.264 and HEVC from the chip get a key frame every two seconds instead of every twelve frames, which looks better at the same size. Where the chip can't do something, the processor does it as before.
+
+- [#90](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/90) [`91b4e90`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/91b4e90c13be6285b03534dfbafe1f028b29d5cc) Thanks [@taavie-z](https://github.com/taavie-z)! - Keep footage audio connected to its own picture in Final Cut Pro exports, preserving source trims, volume and disabled audio. Export separate sound as audio-only clips.
+
+  Match exported audio roles to Studio’s displayed row numbers, including overlapping clips and empty rows, and assign dialogue, effects and music roles to mixed stems. Keep Voice and Footage in separate dialogue subroles so they can be soloed and exported independently.
+
+  Write audio volume keyframes without unsupported interpolation attributes so Final Cut Pro imports faded audio correctly.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - A file whose name has `#`, `?` or `%` in it now plays in a film. Before, `#` made film.html fail to read, and `?` or `%` stopped the preview with an error, or left the file out. film.html writes those three characters escaped (`%23`, `%3F`, `%25`); every other character, Chinese punctuation and spaces included, is written as it is. A src written with escapes, like `my%20file.png`, now names `my file.png` in Studio too. A film's versions also keep such files, and files with quotes in their names.
+
+- [#61](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/61) [`a5b0917`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a5b0917de6bb2fcb490bb65cb957d6c9eadf2c19) Thanks [@taavie-z](https://github.com/taavie-z)! - Export editing projects for Final Cut Pro as FCPXML with their media, the main picture track on the primary storyline, connected overlays and separate audio clips or mixed stems. Separate audio clips retain Studio's row grouping with an audio role per row. HTML pages render as ProRes clips with alpha; subtitles are included as editable title clips above the picture tracks with a separate SRT copy.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - Studio's timeline: the magnet keeps the main track (the lowest picture track) closed up. A clip put on it goes in between, a deleted one closes the gap, and a trim or a new speed moves what follows. Linkage keeps the titles, overlays and sounds over a main-track clip on their moment of it, and deletes them with it; a clip that starts past the end of the main track rides on nothing. Dragging the cut between two clips trims the clip you grab. The new preview axis shows the frame under the pointer as it moves over the timeline; its switch sits in the toolbar beside the magnet, snapping and linkage. Keys: P (or ⇧N) magnet, ~ linkage, S preview axis.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - The main track's head no longer carries a magnet badge, which read as a switch: the main track is told by its band and its tooltip, and the magnet itself is the switch in the timeline's toolbar.
+
+- [#93](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/pull/93) [`a7faee0`](https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm/commit/a7faee013da49aa8415bad2da9f58be26e093fab) Thanks [@PazChen](https://github.com/PazChen)! - A web page picked in the media pane now plays as smoothly as the film in the viewer. The page draws its own frames, one per frame of the project's frame rate, instead of waiting on a message from the editor for each one, so a busy editor no longer makes it stutter. Pausing stops on the frame on screen, and the timecode and the position bar match it.
